INT. PALLIATIVE WARD, ROOM 7 - NIGHT

Dim lamp. Machine hum. A window fogged with breath.

OLD SOLOMON KAPLAN, eighty-three, in the bed, blanket to his chin. A NURSE, forties, in lilac scrubs, checks a drip.

NURSE
Can't sleep?

SOLOMON
I sleep fine.

NURSE
Then why are your eyes open?

SOLOMON
Habit.

She adjusts the pillow. He watches.

SOLOMON (CONT'D)
You new?

NURSE
Six months.

SOLOMON
You'll learn.

NURSE
Learn what?

SOLOMON
Not to look at the clock.

She smiles. She smooths the blanket. She stays.

SOLOMON (CONT'D)
My wife used to hum.

NURSE
What did she hum?

SOLOMON
Whatever was on.

NURSE
Radio?

SOLOMON
Kettle.

A pause.

NURSE
Would you like tea?

SOLOMON
There's no tea at three.

NURSE
There's tea if I say there is.

SOLOMON
What's your name?

NURSE
Danielle.

SOLOMON
Danielle. That's a good name for a nurse.

DANIELLE
Mr. Kaplan, drink some water.

SOLOMON
Solomon.

DANIELLE
Solomon.

He drinks. She holds the cup. His hand shakes on hers.

SOLOMON
My son isn't coming, is he?

DANIELLE
The roads are bad.

SOLOMON
That's a kind lie.

DANIELLE
It's the one I have.

SOLOMON
Keep it.

She sits on the edge of the bed. She doesn't say anything. He closes his eyes. The machine hums on.

SOLOMON (CONT'D)
Hum something.

DANIELLE
I can't carry a tune.

SOLOMON
Neither could she.

Danielle hums. It is not a tune. It is a kettle. His hand slackens on hers, and she keeps humming.
